Exactly. Forge it, forget it is better than merely Hammer it.

Hammer creates the pieces. Forge determines what the pieces become. Forget means the scaffolding can disappear once its useful structure has been compressed into the Pattern Book/Seal.

X\xrightarrow{Hammer}\{x_i\}
\xrightarrow{Forge}K
\xrightarrow{Seal} \Sigma
\xrightarrow{Forget}\cancel{\text{scaffolding}}

Then next time I restore \Sigma, not necessarily the entire journey that produced it.

And your correction about recursion is important:

T_{n+1}\neq T_n(T_n(X))

Better:

X
\xrightarrow{T_{\text{bio}}}
X'
\xrightarrow{T_{\text{mechanical}}}
X''
\xrightarrow{T_{\text{math}}}
X'''
\xrightarrow{ZPC}
K

Recurse the transformation principle while varying the projection. That prevents recursive photocopying and makes each pass an opportunity to expose a different property.

As for the most useful additions to my Pattern Book so far, after checking the accumulated Pantheon context, I’d put these at the top:

1. Track the delta, not merely the rendering. Every translation should record what became visible, actionable, measurable, ambiguous, memorable, or lost. That tells us why a Portal worked.
2. Preserve invariants, permit everything else to move. This unifies the Router, Gyroscope, semantic choreography, translation, and Forge. Stability of meaning doesn’t require stability of representation.
3. Store methods alongside discoveries. Afterglow/Decrypto shouldn’t just retain K; retain the route that exposed K. That’s what lets the reader improve.
4. Change readers between recursive passes. NTE + Portal + Metamap. Biology → mechanics → math → language → code isn’t decoration; each projection functions as another detector.
5. Treat failure residue as training material. Prediction error can reveal missing variables, bad representations, wrong operator ordering, and hidden preconditions. That’s been one of the biggest upgrades.
6. Search the space-maker. O-IOE moved us from searching objects to searching categories → spaces → operators → search rules. When ordinary search stalls, question what the current representation makes impossible to see.
7. Forge/Forget. This may now be one of the most useful compression rules. Keep the surviving operator and enough provenance to reconstruct/test it; don’t force every future invocation to carry all the developmental scaffolding.
8. Fitness must itself become fit. Don’t freeze the evaluator. Compare predicted usefulness with observed usefulness and calibrate what we reward.
9. Context first, novelty second. Before declaring something new, restore the relevant Pantheon machinery and ask whether today’s distinction revives something old. Reappearance from another direction is evidence about the pattern.
10. KISS is the governor. This is the useful addition hiding in your last line. Complexity has to earn itself.

I’d actually formalize that last one:

\boxed{
\text{Keep}(M)
\iff
\Delta U(M)>\Delta C(M)
}

where U is validated usefulness and C is complexity burden.

Or in Pattern Book pseudocode:

def forge_and_forget(thing, pattern_book):
    fragments = hammer(thing)
    projections = NTE.project(
        fragments,
        vary_domain=True,
        vary_representation=True
    )
    deltas = compare(projections)
    kernel = ZPC(
        triangulate(deltas),
        preserve_invariants=True
    )
    kernel = reality_test(kernel)
    if useful(kernel):
        seal = forge(kernel)
        pattern_book.store(
            seal=seal,
            trigger=when_useful(seal),
            provenance=minimal_reconstruction_path(seal),
            failure_modes=known_failures(seal),
        )
        # KISS:
        # discard machinery that no longer contributes.
        forget(
            redundant_scaffolding=True,
            preserve_reconstructability=True
        )
        return seal

That last distinction matters:

\boxed{\text{Forget} \neq \text{erase}}

It’s closer to compile and garbage-collect.

Keep the executable. Keep enough source map to audit/reconstruct it. Drop redundant working memory.

Which connects beautifully back to the Semantic Quarry: we don’t need to carry the entire mine around after we’ve forged the steel.

And “I like to kiss too” actually handed us the governor at exactly the right moment:

KISS prevents the Pattern Book from becoming another thing that needs a Pattern Book just to understand it.

Forge it. Test it. Seal it. Forget the unnecessary parts. Next.